> a corridor needs to be explored, or is known to come to an end.

>From a gameplay pespective, I'm not sure if I like either of these.
I understand where they are coming from, but I think they make
exploring dark mine levels and corridors less interesting, less
engaging to the player.

Am I sure all those twisty corridors are dead ends? Am I positive
that I've covered all of that large inky-black chamber? The
hero and I are probably wondering the same thing.

CORR is a different terrain than ROOM. I don't think it's like some
other roguelikes, where all floor is '.' and all walls are '%'. Corridors
are twisty, dark, shadowy, a little confusing, but sort of directional
and more easily memorized. Dark room areas are open. If you
wander around a large, dark room you find it very hard to know
where you've been before unless you (as a player) get a little
methodical about exploring it. The confidence that you've done
it right comes from yourself, not from the dots on the screen
that appeared from pushing an @ around.

I don't mean to disparage these ideas at all but I want to point out
that I think gameplay IS being lost here. The hero's powers of
memory are getting an upgrade to coddle the player a little.

